            "text": "Pedophilia is a psychiatric and psychological term defined as a primary or exclusive sexual attraction to prepubescent children (typically aged 11 or younger). \n\nIn medical and psychological diagnostic manuals, such as the *Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders* (DSM-5) published by the American Psychiatric Association, it is categorized as a type of **paraphilia** (an atypical sexual interest).\n\n### Diagnostic Distinction\nPsychiatry makes a clear distinction between the paraphilia itself and a mental health disorder:\n\n*   **Pedophilia (Paraphilia):** Refers to the presence of the sexual interest or attraction toward prepubescent children.\n*   **Pedophilic Disorder:** Diagnosed when an individual acts on these urges, or experiences significant personal distress, interpersonal conflict, or impairment in social, occupational, or other important areas of functioning as a result of these attractions.\n\n### Important Legal and Clinical Distinctions\n*   **Attraction vs. Action:** Pedophilia describes a pattern of attraction, whereas child sexual abuse or child sexual exploitation refers to illegal behaviors and criminal acts. While perpetrators of child sexual abuse may have pedophilia, not all abusers meet the clinical criteria for pedophilia, and some individuals with pedophilic attraction do not act on their urges.\n*   **Age of the Child:** Clinically, pedophilia specifically involves attraction to *prepubescent* children. Attraction to post-pubescent adolescents (typically aged 12–17) is referred to separately in clinical literature as *hebephilia* or *ephebophilia*.\n\n### Management and Intervention\nBecause child sexual abuse causes severe, long-lasting harm to victims, mental health organizations and prevention initiatives focus heavily on intervention:\n*   Individuals experiencing these attractions are strongly urged to seek specialized psychological and psychiatric help to manage their thoughts, reduce distress, and prevent any harmful behaviors.\n*   Treatment approaches often involve specialized cognitive-behavioral therapy (CBT) and, in some cases, medications aimed at reducing sex drive to ensure the safety of children and prevent offenses.",
